 "notes": "The SS Maori (1893, 126 x 14.7 m, Wikipedia) wrecked in fog on 5 August 1909 in Maori Bay north of Duiker Point, lying stern inshore with the remains running about 112 m out along 314 degrees magnetic from 7 m at the sternpost to 21 m at the reef off the offshore end. Wreck ends, engine position, anchorage off the split rock and the layout of boilers, pipes, rails, flywheel engines and the 1 m gap under the hull come from the Wikivoyage 'Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/SS Maori' guide; coastline from OpenStreetMap (the BOS 400 wreck, visible above water about 580 m south-west, is left off). Depths between the published 6, 7 and 21 m are interpolated along the hull, the split rock is placed inshore of the stern, and the Oakburn (1906) in the south-east corner of the bay is left off because its position was not found; all positions are approximate.",
